    "text": "All conclusions below are from reading this checkout (`/workspace`); nothing was edited or executed.\n\n## Setup: what `zvec.init(optimize_threads=4)` establishes\n\n`python/zvec/zvec.py:29\u201338,162\u2013163` puts `optimize_threads` into the config dict \u2192 `src/binding/python/model/common/python_config.cc:154\u2013157` (rejects non-positive) \u2192 `src/binding/c/c_api.cc:681\u2013712` (`zvec_initialize`, calling `GlobalConfig::Instance().initialize` at 704) \u2192 `src/db/common/config.cc:121\u2013141` (validate at 83\u201387 rejects 0; `GlobalResource::Instance().initialize()` at 140) \u2192 `src/db/common/global_resource.cc:21\u201333`: the **global optimize pool** is created once (`std::call_once`) as `ailego::ThreadPool(optimize_thread_count(), \u2026)` \u2014 4 workers here, since the constructor clamps size to `[1, hardware_concurrency]` (`src/ailego/parallel/thread_pool.cc:85\u2013101`) and hardware_concurrency \u2265 4 is assumed. Default when unset is `query_thread_count` (`config.cc:42`).\n\n## 1. Pool selection per invocation\n\n`collection.optimize(option)` \u2192 `src/binding/python/model/python_collection.cc:240\u2013247` \u2192 `CollectionImpl::optimize` (`src/db/collection.cc:952`), which passes `options.concurrency_` (`src/include/zvec/db/options.h:60\u201362`) into `build_compact_task` (collection.cc:988\u2013990). The compact path reaches `SegmentImpl::merge_vector_indexer` (segment.cc:1619\u20131620, 1685\u20131686), where the selection happens (`src/db/index/segment/segment.cc:1549\u20131576`; identical logic in `SegmentHelper::MergeWithOptionalReuse`, `src/db/index/segment/segment_helper.cc:799\u2013811`):\n\n- **A (`concurrency=0`)**: `merge_options.pool = GlobalResource::Instance().optimize_thread_pool()` and `write_concurrency = pool->count()` = 4 (segment.cc:1560\u20131564). In the core merge `Index::merge` (`src/core/interface/index.cc:1416\u20131456`), `options.pool != nullptr` \u2192 `reducer->set_thread_pool(options.pool)` (1436\u20131439; setter at `src/include/zvec/core/framework/index_reducer.h:173\u2013184`). So A **borrows the pre-existing global 4-worker optimize pool**; `effective_write_concurrency = min(4, 4) = 4`.\n- **B (`concurrency=2`)**: `merge_options.pool` stays `nullptr`, `write_concurrency = 2` (segment.cc:1565\u20131567). `Index::merge` then creates a **temporary local pool**: `local_thread_pool = make_unique<ailego::ThreadPool>(2, false)` and sets it on the reducer (index.cc:1440\u20131446), with a comment that its lifetime must cover `reduce()` (1433\u20131434). It is destroyed when `Index::merge` returns. Worker count: 2.\n\n**B does not change the global pool.** Nothing on this path writes `GlobalConfig` or `GlobalResource` after init; the global 4-worker pool remains configured (and is created only once via `call_once`, global_resource.cc:22\u201323). A later `concurrency=0` invocation still gets the same 4-worker pool.\n\n## 2. Handoff to the target builder\n\n`MixedStreamerReducer::reduce` requires a non-null pool (mixed_streamer_reducer.cc:211\u2013214) and, when a target builder exists, goes through `reduce_with_builder` (218\u2013223, 672\u2013740) into `index_build` (`src/core/mixed_reducer/mixed_streamer_reducer.cc:742\u2013777`):\n\n- The selected `ailego::ThreadPool` (global pool in A, local pool in B) is wrapped, not copied: `threads = make_shared<BorrowedSingleQueueIndexThreads>(*thread_pool_)` (743\u2013744). This adapter is non-owning and delegates `count()`/`make_group()`/`submit()` to the borrowed pool (`src/include/zvec/core/framework/index_threads.h:174\u2013215`).\n- **Optional conversion training immediately before the builder calls**: `IndexConverter::TrainAndTransform(target_builder_converter_, target_holder, threads)` (745\u2013747), which forwards the same `threads` into `converter->train(two_pass_holder, std::move(threads))` (`src/core/framework/index_converter.cc:27\u201337`, train at 31).\n- **Target builder**: `target_builder_->train(threads, target_holder)` (760) and `target_builder_->build(std::move(threads), target_holder)` (768).\n\nFor DiskANN specifically, the passed pool is genuinely consumed at the graph stages: `build` (diskann_builder.cc:697\u2013790) calls `build_internal(threads)` (762), which submits exactly `threads->count()` worker tasks (304\u2013317), then `prune_internal(threads)` (768) and `generate_quantized_data(threads)` (777). If `threads` were null, `train`/`build` would create their own `SingleQueueIndexThreads(build_thread_count_, false)` (657\u2013660, 714\u2013717).\n\nPassing vs. consuming: the reducer-level handoff above proves the pool is *passed* to the conversion-train and builder train/build calls uniformly, but consumption is implementation-specific \u2014 e.g., `FlatSparseBuilder::train` declares the `IndexThreads::Pointer` parameter unnamed and ignores it (`src/core/algorithm/flat_sparse/flat_sparse_builder.cc:60\u201369`), and `FlatBuilder::train` is a no-op (`src/core/algorithm/flat/flat_builder.h:47\u201351`). Only the per-builder code (like DiskANN's `build_internal` submitting `threads->count()` tasks) proves the workers are actually used.\n\n## 3. DiskANN PQ training: a derived fresh pool, not the caller's\n\n`DiskAnnBuilder::train_quantized_data` (`src/core/algorithm/diskann/diskann_builder.cc:378\u2013427`) does **not** hand the caller's pool to the quantizer. It sizes a new one:\n\n- `build_thread_count_` is the **builder's own setting**, read from param `zvec.diskann.builder.thread_count` (diskann_builder.cc:51; `src/core/algorithm/diskann/diskann_params.h:31\u201332`), defaulting to `hardware_concurrency` when 0/unset (53\u201355).\n- The quantizer's count is clamped by the caller's pool: `pq_thread_count = max(1, min(build_thread_count_, threads->count()))` (391\u2013393), with the in-source comment \"The quantizer creates its own pool, so bound it by the caller's pool\" (390), set as `qp.set(\"thread_count\", pq_thread_count)` (394).\n- `PqInt8Quantizer` stores it (`src/turbo/quantizer/pq_int8_quantizer/pq_int8_quantizer.cc:142`) and, in `train_all_chunks`, **creates a brand-new owned pool**: `make_shared<SingleQueueIndexThreads>(thread_count_, false)` (319\u2013323; this class owns its own `ailego::ThreadPool`, index_threads.h:76\u2013171). PQ chunks are then strided across that pool: worker `i` trains chunks `i, i+pool_count, \u2026` (328\u2013345), invoked from `train` (232, 305).\n\nSo the answer is **no \u2014 it is not reused the same way**. The caller's pool (A: global 4-worker pool; B: local 2-worker pool) influences PQ training only through its `count()`: the fresh PQ pool gets `min(builder setting, caller count)` workers. Under the stated assumptions (param unset \u2192 `build_thread_count_` = hardware_concurrency \u2265 4): A \u2192 4 fresh PQ workers; B \u2192 2 fresh PQ workers, existing *in addition to* the caller's pool threads for the duration of training.\n\n## 4. What the settings establish \u2014 and what they don't\n\n**Established (worker-pool capacity):**\n- The global optimize pool has exactly 4 worker threads (init path above; `ThreadPool` clamps to `[1, hardware_concurrency]`, thread_pool.cc:93\u2013101, and eagerly spawns real `std::thread`s, 103\u2013110).\n- Invocation A's core merge runs on those 4 borrowed workers, with `num_of_add_threads_ = 4` (index.cc:1448\u20131452; used at mixed_streamer_reducer.cc:235\u2013272 for the add-vec fan-out).\n- Invocation B's core merge runs on a temporary 2-worker pool, created and destroyed per `Index::merge` call, leaving the global pool untouched.\n- DiskANN's graph build/prune stages submit `threads->count()` tasks on whichever pool was passed (build_internal, diskann_builder.cc:304\u2013317), and PQ training runs on a fresh pool of `min(build_thread_count_, caller count)` workers (391\u2013394; pq_int8_quantizer.cc:322\u2013323).\n\n**Not established:**\n- **Whole-process thread count.** These settings bound only these pools. Other threads coexist: the global query pool (global_resource.cc:24\u201326), B's per-call local pool plus each PQ-training pool (transient, additive), DiskANN's own fallback pools when `threads` is null (diskann_builder.cc:657\u2013660, 714\u2013717), its IO backend threads, `arrow::compute` initialization threads (segment.cc:68\u201377), and Python/runtime threads. No setting here caps their sum.\n- **Actual CPU utilization.** Pool size is capacity, not occupancy. Pool workers block on an empty queue; `SingleQueueIndexThreads::submit` throttles producers at `kMaxQueueSize = 4096` (index_threads.h:88\u201396, 163); DiskANN's build driver waits on a condition variable while workers run (diskann_builder.cc:317\u2013331); PQ parallelism is additionally capped by `num_chunk_` \u2014 workers beyond the chunk count get no chunks in the strided loop (pq_int8_quantizer.cc:331\u2013345) \u2014 and each chunk's k-means runs inside a single task. Stages like entity loading/serialization are largely sequential. So \"4 optimize threads\" guarantees at most 4 workers in that pool, not 4 busy cores; likewise B's 2 does not mean the process uses exactly 2 threads during optimize."
  },
